"Almost two hundred years after Liu died, the Song dynasty writer Fan Zhongyan praised a friend's calligraphy with four characters: 顏筋柳骨, “the muscle of Yan and the bone of Liu.” The words stuck. Muscle stands for Yan's full, powerful lines; bone stands for Liu's hard, clean ones. Both are praise: the phrase means writing that is strong.": "1b980ecdadc9",
"Ask two friends to write the character 大. They will use the same three strokes in the same order, but the two characters will not look the same. Calligraphers are like that too. Two of the most famous, Yan Zhenqing and Liu Gongquan, both lived in the Tang dynasty and both wrote regular script, yet people can tell their writing apart at a glance.": "5332bbe894bb",
"Ask two friends to write the character 大. They will use the same three strokes in the same order, but the two characters will not look the same. Calligraphers are like that too. Two of the most famous, Yan Zhenqing and Liu Gongquan, both lived in the Tang dynasty and both wrote regular script, yet people can tell their writing apart at a glance. Yan Zhenqing was an official in the 700s. His lines are thick and full, and his characters look broad and steady. He was also known for his courage. When the general An Lushan rebelled, most cities in the north fell, but Yan had quietly repaired the walls of his city, Pingyuan, and stored food, so it was ready. In his seventies he was sent to speak with another rebel, who killed him because he would not give in. Liu Gongquan was born about seventy years later. His lines are leaner, with sharp corners and a tight center, like a strong, thin frame. One day the emperor asked him how to use the brush well. Liu answered, “Using the brush depends on the heart. When the heart is upright, the brush is upright.” The emperor understood that Liu was really giving him advice about how to rule. Almost two hundred years after Liu died, the Song dynasty writer Fan Zhongyan praised a friend's calligraphy with four characters: 顏筋柳骨, “the muscle of Yan and the bone of Liu.” The words stuck. Muscle stands for Yan's full, powerful lines; bone stands for Liu's hard, clean ones. Both are praise: the phrase means writing that is strong. Where does the difference come from? Not from the characters, and not from the stroke order. It comes from how hard the brush presses, where it lifts, how each stroke begins and ends, and how the strokes are spaced. In our model, when both write the same character at the same size, Yan's lines are 1.2 to 1.5 times as thick as Liu's. For more than a thousand years, students have learned regular script by copying masters such as these two, stroke by stroke. People often name four great masters of regular script together: Ouyang Xun, Yan Zhenqing, Liu Gongquan, and Zhao Mengfu. In this lesson, watch two brushes write side by side, guess who wrote each character, and then try both styles yourself.": "ba34a7725bbc",
